          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following is a recognised limitation of chorionic villus sampling c</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following is a recognised limitation of chorionic villus sampling compared to amniocentesis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>CVS cannot provide chromosomal analysis of the fetus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CVS does provide chromosomal analysis because chorionic villi share the same chromosomal makeup as the fetus. Both direct preparation and culture methods yield karyotype results.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CVS carries a risk of confined placental mosaicism and cannot detect open neural tube defects</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Confined placental mosaicism occurs when chromosomal changes are present in the placenta but not the fetus, potentially giving a false-positive result. Since no amniotic fluid is collected, neural tube defects cannot be detected.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CVS results take longer to obtain than amniocentesis results</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CVS direct preparation gives preliminary results in 24–48 hours, which is actually faster than the 10–14 day culture period typically required for amniocentesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CVS can only be performed via the transcervical route</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CVS can be performed either transcervically or transabdominally. In most Indian centres, the transabdominal approach is preferred.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the key differences: CVS samples placental tissue (not amniotic fluid), so mosaicism confined to the placenta can produce a false-positive, and open neural tube defects cannot be detected."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Confined placental mosaicism is a unique pitfall of CVS, and the absence of amniotic fluid means alpha-fetoprotein levels for neural tube defect screening cannot be measured."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
